# The arrow of `later` only calls `f`, but the call that takes the arrow is `push`, out of sight: whoever is called there may keep the arrow, and with it `f`. So `f` does not count as only called: the arrow of `maker` is made in the heap by its own stub, and nothing is asked or moved where `later` makes its arrow. (Once it was lent, and `later` called the run time at every call to bring it to the heap.)
# The arrows are called when the frames of their makers are long gone, behind a collection and a recursion that writes over the stack.
# f1 = later, f3 = maker, f2 = deep
# -- source: capture-that-may-lie-in-a-frame-is-brought-to-the-heap-where-its-closure-is-made-there.ts
